 abstract\n\n... More specifically, our work fits into a bigger context of studying roots for polynomials with integer coefficients in some basis. For example, the roots of Ehrhart polynomials [Beck et al. 2005;Braun and Develin 2008;Bump et al. 2000;Pfeifle 2010], chromatic polynomials [Brenti 1992;Brenti et al. 1994], and Hilbert polynomials [Rodriguez-Villegas 2002] have all been shown to respect similar bounds on the complex plane. ...\n\nCoefficients and Roots of Peak Polynomials\n\nArticle\n\nFull-text available\n\n  * Dec 2015\n  * EXP MATH\n\n  * Sara Billey\n  * Matthew Fahrbach\n  * Alan Talmage\n\nThe lectures - introduce the basic facts about polytopes, with an emphasis on the methods that yield the results (Fourier-Motzkin elimination, Schlegel diagrams, shellability, Gale transforms, and oriented matroids) - discuss important examples and elegant constructions (cyclic and neighborly polytopes, zonotopes, Minkowski sums, permutahedra and associhedra, fiber polytopes, and the Lawrence construction) - show the excitement of current work in the field (Kalai's new diameter bounds, construction of non-rational polytopes, the Bohne-Dress tiling theorem, the upper-bound theorem), and nonextendable shellings) They should provide interesting and enjoyable reading for researchers as well as students.\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nOn the zeros of certain polynomials\n\nArticle\n\n  * Aug 2002\n  * P AM MATH SOC\n\n  * Fernando Rodriguez-Villegas\n\nWe prove that certain naturally arising polynomials have all of their roots on a vertical line. 1. The statement Given a rational function of the form P (t )= U (t)\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nLocation of Zeros of Chromatic and Related Polynomials of Graphs\n\nArticle\n\n  * Nov 2018\n  * CAN J MATH\n\n  * Francesco Brenti\n  * Gordon Royle\n  * David G. Wagner\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\noriented matroid — arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/keyword/oriented%20matroid)\nciteturn14search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; # • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV • ... The classical Descartes' rule of signs bounds the number of positive roots of a real polynomial in one variable by the number of sign changes of its coefficients. ... Our approach starts from the Gale duality and the Khovanskii-Rolle argument of Bihan and Sottile, which reduce the problem to counting common zeros of iterated Jacobians in a polyhedron $Δ$.\n\nDescartes' rule of signs for arbitrary fewnomial systems\n\nThe classical Descartes' rule of signs bounds the number of positive roots of a real polynomial in one variable by the number of sign changes of its coefficients. This bound only depends on the order of the exponents and on the signs of the coefficients, that is, on the oriented matroids of the exponent and coefficient matrices. We give a multivariate generalization of this rule to arbitrary fewnomial systems: for systems of $n$ real polynomial equations in $n$ variables with $n+k+1$ monomials, we obtain upper bounds for the number of nondegenerate positive solutions which only depend on sign conditions on the maximal minors of the coefficient matrix and of the exponent matrix and its liftings. For $k=1$ they are the Descartes rules of signs for circuits of Bihan-Dickenstein and Bihan-Dickenstein-Forsgård. Our approach starts from the Gale duality and the Khovanskii-Rolle argument of Bihan and Sottile, which reduce the problem to counting common zeros of iterated Jacobians in a polyhedron $Δ$. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Auel_A in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AAuel_A+in%3Amath)\nciteturn14search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Sep 10 2026 math.AG math.NT arXiv:2609.09351v1 ... We compute its geometric automorphism group via an argument involving Gale duality. ... @misc{2507.22138, author = {Gaik Ambartsoumian and Asher Auel and Mohammad Javad Latifi Jebelli}, title = {{S}ymmetric star transforms and the algebraic geometry of their dual differential operators}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2507.22138}, note = {arXiv:2507.22138v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\n32 results for au:Auel_A in:math\n\nShow all abstracts\n\n  * The algebraic geometry of 3-by-3 magic squares of squares\n\nAsher Auel, Benjamin Singer\n\nSep 10 2026 math.AG math.NT arXiv:2609.09351v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2609.09351, author = {Asher Auel and Benjamin Singer}, title = {{T}he algebraic geometry of 3-by-3 magic squares of squares}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2609.09351}, note = {arXiv:2609.09351v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nThe question of whether a 3-by-3 magic square of squares with distinct integer entries exists has been open since the 18th century. We study the geometry of the algebraic surface parameterizing 3-by-3 magic squares of squares, which is a singular complete intersection of six quadrics in projective 8-space. We compute its geometric automorphism group via an argument involving Gale duality. We compute the basic topological invariants and Hodge diamond of its resolution. We provide an explicit rank 518 sublattice of its geometric Picard group, close to the Hodge-theoretic upper bound of 544. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[arch-commits] Commit in sagemath/repos/community-x86_64 (32 files) (https://lists.archlinux.org/pipermail/arch-commits/2018-November/556820.html)\nciteturn14search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.0 years ago; Crawled: 5.3 years ago;     -             sage: p = P.ehrhart_polynomial(maxdet=5, verbose=True)  # optional - latte_int\n    -             sage: p = P.ehrhart_polynomial(irrational_primal=True, verbose=True)   # optional - latte_int\n    -             This is LattE integrale ...\n    -             ...\n    --            Invocation: count --ehrhart-polynomial '--redundancy-check=none' --cdd --irrational-primal /dev/stdin\n    -+